WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.116
This is Read Tabs, Play Guitar, by Fretbox. Guitar tab and notation, one step at a time.

00:00:07.916 --> 00:00:10.653
Today: scales and practising with tabs.

00:00:12.453 --> 00:00:15.184
Scales. The notes that belong together.

00:00:15.984 --> 00:00:26.722
A scale is a set of notes, ordered by pitch, that a melody or a solo can draw from. On the guitar, scales are learned as shapes: finger patterns that you can move up and down the neck.

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:29.223
The major scale.

00:00:30.023 --> 00:00:44.243
The major scale, the familiar do re mi fa sol la ti do, follows a fixed pattern of steps: whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. A whole step is two frets; a half step is one.

00:00:45.043 --> 00:00:58.074
You can hear the pattern on a single string. Starting on string 5, fret 3 (C), play the frets below. These are the notes C, D, E, F, G, A, B, C:

00:00:58.874 --> 00:01:00.865
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:12.378
Playing along one string makes the pattern easy to see. Playing across the strings, in one position, is easier for your hand. Here is the same C major scale in one position:

00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:15.169
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:16.669 --> 00:01:18.368
The minor pentatonic scale.

00:01:19.168 --> 00:01:33.419
The minor pentatonic has five notes instead of seven. It is the scale behind a great many rock, blues and pop solos, and its most common shape, often called the first “box”, is one of the first things guitarists learn to improvise with.

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:41.784
Here is A minor pentatonic at the fifth fret. Use your first finger for fret 5 and your third or fourth finger for fret 7 and 8:

00:01:42.584 --> 00:01:44.575
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:45.375 --> 00:01:58.966
The notes are A, C, D, E and G. The A notes, the root, are on string 6 fret 5, string 4 fret 7 and string 1 fret 5. Ending a phrase on the root sounds resolved.

00:02:00.466 --> 00:02:01.512
Moving shapes.

00:02:02.312 --> 00:02:16.461
Because every fret is a semitone, a shape works in any key. Move the whole minor pentatonic box up two frets, with string 6 now starting at fret 7, and you are playing in B minor. Move it down to fret 3 and you are in G minor.

00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:28.920
Tip: Play scales with a metronome, and always alternate your pick strokes: down, up, down, up. Even, clean notes at a slow tempo are worth more than fast, uneven ones.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.141
Practice.

00:02:31.941 --> 00:02:38.151
First: play the C major scale on one string, then in one position. Sing along if you can.

00:02:38.951 --> 00:02:44.461
Second: learn the A minor pentatonic box going up and down, slowly, with a metronome.

00:02:45.261 --> 00:02:52.337
Third: improvise for two minutes using only the box, over a backing track in A minor, or over the chords Am and G.

00:02:54.137 --> 00:02:57.706
Practising with tabs. From reading a song to playing it.

00:02:58.506 --> 00:03:07.834
Finding a tab is easy. Turning it into a performance takes a method. This chapter is about that method, and about getting the most from tab files and a tab player.

00:03:09.334 --> 00:03:10.617
Check the tab first.

00:03:11.417 --> 00:03:18.798
Free tabs on the internet are often written by ear by other players, and quality varies a lot. Before you put hours into one:

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:22.873
Listen to the recording while you read the tab. Does it match?

00:03:23.673 --> 00:03:28.277
Check the tuning noted at the top. Some songs use dropped or lowered tunings.

00:03:29.077 --> 00:03:36.581
Look for a capo indication. A capo raises every open string, and the fret numbers in the tab are usually counted from the capo.

00:03:38.081 --> 00:03:39.670
Tab files and players.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:59.478
Text tabs are plain text. Tab files made in programs such as Guitar Pro, or saved in the open MusicXML format, contain much more: the rhythm, several instruments, the tempo, and the techniques. A tab player shows these files as notation and tab and plays them back, so you can hear what you read.

00:04:00.278 --> 00:04:02.445
The features that matter most for practice:

00:04:03.245 --> 00:04:07.401
Slow down: play the song at a lower tempo without changing its pitch.

00:04:08.201 --> 00:04:12.790
Loop: repeat a short section, for example two bars, again and again.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:18.175
Follow along: a cursor that shows where you are, so your eyes and ears stay together.

00:04:18.975 --> 00:04:23.972
Mute or solo tracks: hear only the guitar part, or play along with the band without it.

00:04:25.472 --> 00:04:26.547
A practice method.

00:04:27.347 --> 00:04:32.131
First: listen. Play the song a few times and follow the tab with your eyes.

00:04:32.931 --> 00:04:40.913
Second: split it up. Divide it into sections: intro, verse, chorus, solo. Work on one at a time.

00:04:41.713 --> 00:04:50.522
Third: loop and slow down. Set a short loop at a tempo where you can play it cleanly, even if that is 50% or 60% of the original speed.

00:04:51.322 --> 00:04:59.929
Fourth: speed up gradually. When it is clean several times in a row, raise the tempo a little. If it falls apart, go back one step.

00:05:00.729 --> 00:05:06.998
Fifth: join the pieces. Play two sections in a row, then three, until you can play the whole song.

00:05:07.798 --> 00:05:12.428
Sixth: play with the recording. Finally, play along with the real song.

00:05:13.228 --> 00:05:21.509
Tip: Short, regular sessions beat rare, long ones. Twenty focused minutes every day will take you further than three hours once a week.

00:05:23.009 --> 00:05:23.730
Practice.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:28.226
First: choose a song a little above your level and find a tab for it.

00:05:29.026 --> 00:05:33.022
Second: check it against the recording, and note the tuning and any capo.

00:05:33.822 --> 00:05:38.351
Third: learn the first section with the method above, starting at a slow tempo.

00:05:39.151 --> 00:05:44.091
Fourth: write down your tempo each day. Watching it rise is a good motivation.

00:05:45.891 --> 00:05:46.952
That's all for today.

00:05:47.752 --> 00:05:48.954
If you want to keep going:

00:05:49.754 --> 00:06:01.167
Fretbox is the app behind the ebook. It is a tab player for guitarists: it opens tablature files in the Guitar Pro and MusicXML formats, so you can read a song and hear it at the same time.

00:06:01.967 --> 00:06:04.604
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be used with it:

00:06:05.404 --> 00:06:08.164
reading tablature and the symbols for each technique.

00:06:08.964 --> 00:06:11.563
following the rhythm in the notation that comes with the tab.

00:06:12.363 --> 00:06:15.094
learning the fretboard, chords and scales.

00:06:15.894 --> 00:06:20.208
practising song by song, section by section, at your own pace.

00:06:21.008 --> 00:06:25.153
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who is starting out on the guitar.

00:06:25.953 --> 00:06:30.514
This episode of Read Tabs, Play Guitar was narrated with a synthetic voice.

